    {
      "filename": "Imposing entrance arch to the Town Hall- Partition Museum in Amritsar (24074291387).jpg",
      "title": "File:Imposing entrance arch to the Town Hall- Partition Museum in Amritsar (24074291387).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/21/Imposing_entrance_arch_to_the_Town_Hall-_Partition_Museum_in_Amritsar_%2824074291387%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Imposing_entrance_arch_to_the_Town_Hall-_Partition_Museum_in_Amritsar_(24074291387).jpg",
      "width": 4032,
      "height": 3024,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "shankar s. from Dubai, united arab emirates",
      "description": "It's a brand new, and breakkie done, the order of business for this morning is a heritage walk into the congested old town district of Amritsar. Our guide will meet us near the Amritsar Town Hall, of which this is one of the portals. This long shot taken by my brother shows my sister and I walking under the portals, accompanied by our Sikh guide. My brother joined us soon after taking this picture. The Amritsar Town Hall was built by the British in 1866, and was the then centre of the Amritsar's local administration. (Amritsar, Punjab, northern India, Nov. 2017)",
